{"profiles":[{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Grass Parrots","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Neophema","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Neophema Salvadori, 1891","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2479503},"ottId":145557,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Neophema","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The genus Neophema is an Australian genus with six or seven species. They are small, dull green parrots differentiated by patches of other colours, and are commonly known as grass parrots.","revisionId":1289567350,"title":"Neophema","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neophema","wikidataId":"Q1049046"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Peruvian Squirrel Monkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Black-capped squirrel monkey","source":"Wikipedia"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Saimiri boliviensis peruviensis","confidence":98,"rank":"subspecies","scientificName":"Saimiri boliviensis peruviensis Hershkovitz, 1984","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":4266901},"ottId":390933,"rank":"subspecies","scientificName":"Saimiri boliviensis peruviensis","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The black-capped squirrel monkey (Saimiri boliviensis) is a species of New-World monkey native to the upper Amazon basin in Bolivia, western Brazil and eastern Peru. They weigh between 365 and 1,135 g (13 and 40 oz) and measure, from the head to the base of the tail, between 225 and 370 mm (9 and 15 in).","revisionId":1360620016,"title":"Black-capped squirrel monkey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black-capped_squirrel_monkey","wikidataId":"Q834329"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Crowned Lemur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Eulemur coronatus","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Eulemur coronatus (Gray, 1842)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436422},"ottId":394965,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Eulemur coronatus","synonyms":["Lemur chrysampyx","Lemur coronatus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The crowned lemur (Eulemur coronatus) is a lemur that is 31–36 cm (12–14 in) long and weighs 2 kg (4.4 lb). Its tail is about 42–51 cm (17–20 in) long.","revisionId":1356979775,"title":"Crowned lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crowned_lemur","wikidataId":"Q151910"}},{"commonNames":[],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Indri","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Indri É.Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire & G.Cuvier, 1796","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436431},"ottId":696021,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Indri","synonyms":["Indris","Indrium","Lichanatus","Lichanotus","Pithelemur"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The indri ( ; Indri indri), also called the babakoto, is one of the largest living lemurs, with a head-body length of about 64–72 cm (25–28+1⁄2 in) and a weight of between 6 and 9.5 kg (13 and 21 lb). It has a black and white coat and maintains an upright posture when climbing or clinging.","revisionId":1376950353,"title":"Indri","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indri","wikidataId":"Q203868"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Skimmers","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Rynchops","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Rynchops Linnaeus, 1758","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2481206},"ottId":738517,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Rynchops","synonyms":["Rhijncops","Rhinchops","Rhincops","Rhynchops","Rhynchopsalis","Rhyncops"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The skimmers, forming the genus Rynchops (from Ancient Greek ῥύγχος (rhúnkhos), meaning \"beak\", and ὄψ (óps), meaning \"face\"), are tern-like birds in the family Laridae. The genus comprises three species found in South Asia, Africa, and the Americas.","revisionId":1353132989,"title":"Rynchops","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rynchops","wikidataId":"Q212941"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"African and Mascarene Sheldgeese","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Alopochen","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","sci